; A trait-method IMPLEMENTATION can declare its effects, and before
; 2026-08-25 it was the one declaration in the language that could not.
;
; Two bugs sat between the author and the answer, and the first hid
; the second. `attachAxtags` walked only the TOP-LEVEL declaration
; vector, so a tag written above `(emit ...)` inside an `(impl ...)`
; fell between the impl's span and the next top-level declaration's
; and was handed to that declaration, or dropped. And the expander
; LOWERS each impl method into a fresh `TAG_D_FN` named
; `Trait#Type#method` - the node everything after expansion actually
; checks - copying the member's span but not its tags, so even an
; attached tag died at the lowering.
;
; Both are fixed here: the cursor threads through an impl's methods,
; and the lowered node inherits word 7. A diagnostic no edit can
; satisfy is worse than no diagnostic, and the fix for one is a place
; to put the answer rather than silence about the question.
;
; `speaks` is the whole point: it is INSIDE the impl and it works.
; `quiet` is the control - the same shape with no tag - and it draws
; AX3042 at the mangled name, which is what proves the check reaches
; impl members at all rather than skipping them.
(trait (Loud a) where (
  speaks :: (-> a Int)
))

(trait (Hush a) where (
  quiet :: (-> a Int)
))

(impl (Loud Int) where (
  ;@axiom:effect(io)
  (speaks
    (lambda (x) (__syscall3 1 1 x 0))
  )
))

(impl (Hush Int) where (
  (quiet
    (lambda (x) (__syscall3 1 1 x 0))
  )
))

(:: caller (-> Int Int))

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (caller n) (speaks n))

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main) (caller 1))
